---
import SimplePage from '../../../components/content/SimplePage.astro';
import { getCtaLinks } from '../../../lib/cms.ts';
const { bookUrl } = await getCtaLinks();
---

<SimplePage
  title="Moving to Perth"
  description="A practical guide to settling in Perth, Western Australia: suburbs, schools, cost of living, Medicare, banking and getting set up — from a Perth-based migration practice."
  eyebrow="Life stage"
  lead="What life is actually like once the visa is sorted — a practical guide to settling in Perth, Western Australia."
  cta={{ href: bookUrl, label: 'Book a Consultation' }}
  disclaimer={true}
>
  <p>
    Perth is Western Australia's capital — a calm, spacious city on the Swan River with a
    Mediterranean climate, some of the world's most liveable suburbs, and a strong economy built on
    resources, health, education and construction. For many new arrivals it offers a gentler pace
    than the eastern capitals, with beaches, parks and good schools close to the CBD.
  </p>

  <h2>Choosing where to live</h2>
  <p>
    Perth's suburbs each have their own character — riverside and coastal areas, established
    tree-lined suburbs near the city, and newer, more affordable family suburbs further out. When
    you're choosing, weigh up your commute, school catchments, and whether you want to be near the
    coast, the river or the hills. Renting first while you get to know the city is common and
    sensible.
  </p>

  <h2>Schools</h2>
  <p>
    Western Australia has public (government) schools, Catholic schools and independent schools.
    Public school enrolment is generally tied to where you live (the local intake area), so school
    catchments are worth checking before you settle on a suburb. International students and some
    visa holders may pay fees at government schools — we can point you to the current rules for your
    visa.
  </p>

  <h2>Cost of living</h2>
  <p>
    Your main costs will be rent, groceries, transport, utilities and health cover. Perth is
    generally more affordable than Sydney or Melbourne, though costs vary by suburb and lifestyle.
    Budget for a rental bond and initial set-up costs when you arrive, and allow time to build a
    local rental and credit history.
  </p>

  <h2>Health cover &amp; Medicare</h2>
  <p>
    Access to Medicare (Australia's public health system) depends on your visa. Permanent residents
    and some visa holders can enrol; temporary visa holders, including students, generally need
    private health cover (such as OSHC for students) for the duration of their stay. Confirm what
    applies to your visa before you rely on public cover.
  </p>

  <h2>Getting set up</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Open an Australian bank account (you can often start this before you arrive).</li>
    <li>Apply for a Tax File Number (TFN) once you're here if you'll be working.</li>
    <li>Sort a mobile plan and, once you have a lease, connect electricity, gas and internet.</li>
    <li>Look into public transport (Transperth) and whether you'll need a car for your suburb.</li>
    <li>Register with a local GP and, if eligible, enrol in Medicare.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    This is general information to help you plan — the details that matter most depend on your visa.
    If you're moving to Perth, or planning to, we're a Perth-based practice and happy to help you
    understand what your visa allows and what to prepare.
  </p>
</SimplePage>
